{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_7qor7u4zgqel6maf3ttnqn275izoml3qohsie7cyyddukgek3ujq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_012VkFe4ZHSeTDY7iqEB26oA","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Translate BF96 dtoa_r path to Rust","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"You must fix a C-to-Rust translation defect. The C code is GROUND TRUTH and must be replicated exactly. Do NOT modify anything under `c_src/`.\n\n## Working dir\n/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-QfYM1w/translated_rust\n\n## The defect\nThe Rust file `src/dtoa.rs` translated the WRONG `#ifdef` branch of `dtoa_r`.\n\nIts header comment (lines 4-8) explicitly says it implemented \"the *classic Bigint* code path (equivalent to compiling without USE_BF96)\" and ASSUMED that produces byte-identical output to the BF96 path. THAT ASSUMPTION IS FALSE.\n\nIn the real C build, `USE_BF96` **IS** defined (dtoa.c line 385: `#define USE_BF96`, reached because neither `NO_LONG_LONG` nor `NO_BF96` is defined). So the C compiles a COMPLETELY DIFFERENT algorithm: an exact-integer / 96-bit-bigfloat fast path using `pten`/`Lhint`/`pfive`/`pfivebits`/`dtoa_divmax`, with labels `use_exact`, `no_div`, `one_digit`, `no_digits`, `toobig`, and variables `dbits`, `dbhi`, `dblo`, `res`, `ulp`, `ulplo`, `ulpshift`, `ulpadj`, `eulp`, `den`, `rb`, `zb`, `sres`, `sulp`, `ures`, `tv0..tv3`.\n\nCorroborating evidence: `cargo build` warns that `PTEN`, `LHINT`, `BF96`, and `PFIVEBITS` are **never used** in the Rust — precisely the BF96 tables the real C path depends on.\n\n## Observed divergences (differential test vs the C .so)\nCalling the exported `dtoa_r(double, int mode, int ndigits, int *decpt, int *sign, char **rve, char *buf, size_t blen)` directly:\n\n1. mode 1, bits 0x44b52d02c7e14af6:\n   C:    decpt=24, sign=0, digits \"1\"\n   Rust: decpt=23, sign=0, digits \"9999999999999999\"\n2. mode 4, ndigits=17, bits 0x3fb999999999999a (0.1):\n   C:    decpt=0, digits \"10000000000000001\"\n   Rust: decpt=0, digits \"1\"\n   ndigits=18 -> C \"100000000000000006\"; 19 -> C \"1000000000000000056\"; 20 -> C \"10000000000000000555\"\n3. mode 4, ndigits=17, bits 0x400921fb54442d18 (pi):\n   C:    decpt=1, digits \"31415926535897931\"\n   Rust: decpt=1, digits \"3141592653589793\"\n   ndigits=18 -> C \"314159265358979312\"; 19 and 20 -> C \"3141592653589793116\"\n4. mode 5 with ndigits>=16 diverges similarly.\nModes 0, 2, 3 and the whole `json_dumps` real-number path currently MATCH and must KEEP matching.\n\n## Ground truth source (use this!)\nI have preprocessed the C with the exact build flags so all `#ifdef`s are already resolved. The ACTIVE body of `dtoa_r` — the real 673 lines that compile — is saved at:\n\n    /tmp/dtoa_r_active.c\n\nREAD THAT FILE FIRST. It is the definitive specification. It was produced with:\n    gcc -E -P -DHAVE_CONFIG_H -Ic_src/include -Ic_src/src c_src/src/dtoa.c\nthen extracting the `dtoa_r` function. Macro names are expanded (e.g. `word0(&u)` appears as `(&u)->L[1]`, `Exp_mask` as `0x7ff00000`, `Bias` as `0x3ff`). For readable macro names cross-reference the original `c_src/src/dtoa.c` (function starts line 5012; USE_BF96 blocks at 5072-5139, 5154-5294, 5367+, 5779+ are ACTIVE; blocks guarded `#ifndef USE_BF96` at 5139, 5294, 5306, 5813 are DEAD).\n\nNote these are NOT defined in the real build, so their branches are dead: `Sudden_Underflow`, `SET_INEXACT`, `Honor_FLT_ROUNDS`, `ROUND_BIASED`, `Check_FLT_ROUNDS`, `No_leftright`, `NO_BF96`, `NO_LONG_LONG`, `Just_16`, `VAX`, `IBM`, `IEEE_MC68k`. `IEEE_8087` (little-endian) IS defined. Also note `try_quick` does NOT exist in the active path at all.\n\n## Your task\nRewrite the body of `dtoa_r` in `src/dtoa.rs` (currently at line ~933, `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub unsafe extern \"C\" fn dtoa_r`) to be a FAITHFUL, line-by-line translation of the active BF96 code in `/tmp/dtoa_r_active.c`.\n\nKeep the existing exported signature EXACTLY as-is:\n```rust\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\npub unsafe extern \"C\" fn dtoa_r(\n    dd: f64, mut mode: c_int, mut ndigits: c_int,\n    decpt: *mut c_int, sign: *mut c_int, rve: *mut *mut c_char,\n    buf_in: *mut c_char, blen_in: usize,\n) -> *mut c_char\n```\n\n### Helpers that ALREADY EXIST in src/dtoa.rs — reuse them, do not rewrite:\n```rust\nunsafe fn multadd(b: *mut Bigint, m: c_int, a: c_int) -> *mut Bigint   // line 204\nunsafe fn i2b(i: c_int) -> *mut Bigint                                  // line 348\nunsafe fn mult(a: *mut Bigint, b: *mut Bigint) -> *mut Bigint           // line 355\nunsafe fn pow5mult(b: *mut Bigint, k: c_int) -> *mut Bigint             // line 419\nunsafe fn lshift(b: *mut Bigint, k: c_int) -> *mut Bigint               // line 455\nunsafe fn cmp(a: *mut Bigint, b: *mut Bigint) -> c_int                  // line 504\nunsafe fn diff(a: *mut Bigint, b: *mut Bigint) -> *mut Bigint           // line 529\nunsafe fn d2b(d: &U, e: &mut c_int, bits: &mut c_int) -> *mut Bigint     // line 642\nfn dshift(b: *mut Bigint, p2: c_int) -> c_int                           // line 783\nunsafe fn quorem(b: *mut Bigint, s: *mut Bigint) -> c_int               // line 793\nunsafe fn rv_alloc(i: c_int) -> *mut c_char                             // line 871\nunsafe fn nrv_alloc(s: *const c_char, s0: *mut c_char, s0len: usize,\n                    rve: *mut *mut c_char, n: c_int) -> *mut c_char      // line 886\n```\nAlso available: `struct U` (line 80) with `.d()`, `.set_d()`, `.word0()`, `.word1()`; `struct Bigint` (line 130); `bfree`; tables `PFIVE` (line 728, `[u64;27]`), `PFIVEBITS` (line 758, `[c_int;25]`), and from `dtoa_tables`: `BF96 { b0: u32, b1: u32, b2: u32, e: c_int }`, `PTEN: [BF96;667]`, `LHINT: [i16;2098]`, plus `pub static mut dtoa_divmax: c_int` (line 25).\n\nIf `U` lacks a raw 64-bit accessor (the C uses `u.LL`), add a small helper method (e.g. `fn ll(&self)->u64` / `set_ll`) via `f64::to_bits`/`from_bits` — do NOT change `U`'s layout.\n\n### Translation requirements (critical for byte-identical behavior)\n- The C uses `goto` heavily (`use_exact`, `no_div`, `one_digit`, `no_digits`, `toobig`, `ret`, `retc`, `rete`, `bump_up`, `Roundup`, ...). Emulate this control flow EXACTLY, e.g. with a `loop { match state { ... } }` state machine or nested labeled blocks (`'label: loop`/`break 'label`). Do not \"simplify\" or restructure the logic — subtle fallthrough is load-bearing.\n- All the `unsigned long long` arithmetic (`dbits`, `res`, `ulp`, `den`, `rb`, `zb`, `sres`, `sulp`, `ures`, `tv0..tv3`, `hb`, `reslo`, `ureslo`, `res0`, `res3`, `ulpmask`) must be `u64` with WRAPPING semantics where C would wrap. Shifts must match C exactly (watch `1ull << n`).\n- Signed `int` arithmetic stays `c_int` (i32).\n- Preserve the exact `*decpt` and `*sign` writes, the `*rve` write, the trailing-zero stripping, and the `buf`/`blen` handling (including the `blen <= (size_t)i` early-return that returns NULL and sets `*rve = buf + i` where `buf` was just set to 0 — replicate this quirk literally, including the resulting `*rve` value).\n- Keep the `#[unsafe(no_mangle)]` attribute style already used in the file (this crate is on nightly and uses `#[unsafe(no_mangle)]`).\n- Preserve the existing `dtoa` wrapper (line ~1656) which forwards to `dtoa_r`, and `freedtoa`, `gethex`, `strtod__unused` exports.\n- If parts of the old classic-path code become dead, you may delete them, BUT keep any function still referenced by other modules or by the remaining exports (`gethex`, `strtod__unused`, `jsonp_strtod` in `src/dtoa_strtod.rs`/`src/strconv.rs`). Check with grep before deleting anything.\n\n### Verify your work\n```\ncd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-QfYM1w/translated_rust\ncargo build --release 2>&1 | tail -20\ngcc -O1 -o /tmp/dd tests_c/diff_driver.c -ldl\n/tmp/dd $PWD/cbuild/libjansson.so > /tmp/c.txt\n/tmp/dd $PWD/target/release/libjansson.so > /tmp/r.txt\ndiff /tmp/c.txt /tmp/r.txt | head -40\ndiff /tmp/c.txt /tmp/r.txt | grep -c '^[<>]'\n```\nBoth files are 75420 lines. The baseline BEFORE your fix has 232 differing lines. Your goal is **0 differing lines**. Iterate until the diff is empty. Report the final differing-line count honestly — do not claim success without running this comparison."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1925,"cache_read_input_tokens":65590,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1925,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c1ef89ae-e696-4035-a7a9-923ffb551939","uuid":"bea37a92-6cc6-4deb-8c94-3a473fc8eed3","timestamp":"2026-08-14T14:53:49.154Z"}
