{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_m2gyllrnvkuqssy3wevdiawpwek6bf47dtv2yntbubj4x5yjokqa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All three Phase A artifacts complete. Now let me write the core Phase B differential tests myself."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1401,"cache_read_input_tokens":198510,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1401,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c1ef89ae-e696-4035-a7a9-923ffb551939","uuid":"8b7ef746-d1db-4296-a812-a19c22d9e7e0","timestamp":"2026-08-14T15:06:21.837Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_xdnvphkzf56moeo5litjzpcvwvyux25kiedbw6rwjcqkudxgv2la","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. `src/dtoa.rs` now implements the real (USE_BF96) `dtoa_r`, and the differential test is clean.\n\n## What was wrong\nThe Rust had translated the `#ifndef USE_BF96` classic-Bigint branch (including `try_quick`, `k_check`, `d2` / `eps` floating-point estimation). In the real build `USE_BF96` **is** defined (dtoa.c:385), so the C runs a different algorithm. The old header comment's claim that both paths are byte-identical is false: the BF96 path's `use_exact` / `no_div` exact-integer loops and its 61-/96-bit `res`/`ulp`/`eulp` loops have their own stopping rules that produce different digit strings for modes 1, 4 and 5.\n\n## What I did\n- Replaced the whole body of `dtoa_r` (`src/dtoa.rs`, now at line ~959) with a line-by-line translation of `/tmp/dtoa_r_active.c`:\n  - `dbits`/`be`/`ulpadj` denormal normalisation, `Lhint`/`pten` seeding of `k`, the mode switch, `buf`/`blen` handling (including the literal `buf = 0; *rve = buf + i` quirk), and `spec_case`.\n  - All the `goto` targets (`use_exact`, `use_exact1`, `no_div`, `ulp_reached`, `Roundup`, `toobig`, `Fast_failed`, `Fast_failed1`, `more96`, `no_digits`, `one_digit`, `round_9_up`, `accept_dig`, `roundoff`, `ret`, `retc`, `ret1`) are emulated exactly by a `'sm: loop { match st { … } }` state machine, so the fallthrough and mid-loop entries are preserved. `goto more96` is a `break 'blk` out of a labeled block.\n  - All `unsigned long long` arithmetic is `u64` with `wrapping_*`; variable shift counts go through two new helpers `sl`/`sr` (`wrapping_shl`/`wrapping_shr`) so they match x86-64's mod-64 shift behaviour where C would be UB.\n  - `switch(Rounding)` (with `Rounding` expanding to `1`, no matching case) is a documented no-op.\n  - Renamed the local `ulp` to `ulpv` and `S` to `sbig` to avoid clashing with the existing `fn ulp`.\n- Deleted the now-dead tail helpers `ret_ret`/`retc_ret`/`finish_ret`/`no_digits_ret`/`bump_up_retc`/`roundoff_ret`/`mlo_none` (grep confirmed nothing else referenced them). Kept `b2d`, `ratio`, `ulp`, `s2b`, `TENS`/`BIGTENS`/`TINYTENS`, `HEXDIG` etc. and added `#![allow(dead_code)]`, since `gethex`/`strtod__unused`/`jsonp_strtod` are still stubs that will need them.\n- Rewrote the misleading module header comment; dropped the now-unused `BF96` import. `PTEN`, `LHINT`, `PFIVE`, `PFIVEBITS` are all used now — the \"never used\" warnings are gone.\n- All exports preserved: `dtoa_r`, `dtoa`, `freedtoa`, `gethex`, `strtod__unused`, `dtoa_divmax`, `jsonp_strtod` (verified with `nm`).\n\n## Verification\n- **Primary: `diff /tmp/c.txt /tmp/r.txt` → 0 differing lines** (both files 75420 lines; baseline was 232).\n- The four reported divergences now match the C exactly (e.g. mode 1 `0x44b52d02c7e14af6` → `decpt=24 \"1\"`; mode 4 nd=17 for 0.1 → `\"10000000000000001\"`; pi nd=17..20 → `31415926535897931`/`314159265358979312`/`3141592653589793116`).\n- **Extra sweep I wrote** (3.7M lines): 6000 random doubles × modes 0-9 × ndigits -30..30, powers of ten × modes 0-9 × ndigits -25..25, the `buf == NULL` path via `dtoa` + `freedtoa`, and the small-`blen` NULL-return path (checking `*rve`) → 0 differences after normalising the printed raw stack pointer (my harness printed `rve` as an absolute address, which differs between processes; `rve - rv` and everything else match).\n\n## One deliberate deviation, worth knowing\nAt dtoa.c:5408 the C evaluates `pfive[k - 1]` and `k == 0` is reachable (mode 3/5 with `ndigits == -1` and `1 <= |d| < 10`), i.e. C reads out of bounds. I added `pfive_at()` which returns `0` for a negative index instead of reading OOB, with a comment. I tested that corner explicitly (`d = 1.0, 5.0, 9.0, 9.5` with mode 3/5, `ndigits = -1`) and Rust matches C on all of them, and jansson never calls `dtoa_r` with negative `ndigits`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":234,"cache_read_input_tokens":138601,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":234,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_012VkFe4ZHSeTDY7iqEB26oA","session_id":"c1ef89ae-e696-4035-a7a9-923ffb551939","uuid":"f47ab34c-f6fb-4dd1-8b79-c61fdf3c1db3","timestamp":"2026-08-14T15:06:37.674Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Translate BF96 dtoa_r path to Rust"}
