phase_b_lowlevel.rs`\n- Do NOT modify anything else. Especially not `c_src/`, `src/`, `tests/common/mod.rs`, `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md`, `Cargo.toml`.\n- The C code is GROUND TRUTH. If C and Rust disagree, DO NOT fix the Rust and DO NOT weaken the test. REPORT the divergence precisely.\n- NEVER call Rust functions directly — everything through the loaded `.so` exports.\n\n## Harness available (`tests/common/mod.rs`) — begin each file with `mod common;` then `use common::*;`\n```rust\nlibs() -> &'static Libs           // Libs { c: Library, r: Library }, both pre-seeded via json_object_seed\nunsafe sym::<T>(&Library, \"name\") -> libloading::Symbol<T>\ndiff(label: &str, f: impl Fn(&Library) -> T)                 // T: PartialEq+Debug\ndiff_n(label: &str, n: u64, f: impl Fn(&Library, u64) -> T)\n```\n`json_t { type_: c_int, refcount: usize }` (you can read `.type_` and `.refcount` to assert them — do so, refcount behavior is observable).\nConstants: type tags `JSON_OBJECT JSON_ARRAY JSON_STRING JSON_INTEGER JSON_REAL JSON_TRUE JSON_FALSE JSON_NULL`; flags `JSON_SORT_KEYS JSON_COMPACT JSON_ENCODE_ANY JSON_ENSURE_ASCII`, `json_indent(n)`, `json_real_precision(n)`.\nHelpers: `decref(lib, ptr)`, `incref(ptr)`, `dumps_to_string(lib, ptr, flags) -> Option<String>`, `cs(\"s\") -> CString`, `cs_bytes(&[u8]) -> Vec<u8>`, `cstr_to_string(ptr)`, `libc_free(ptr)`, `Rng::new(seed)` with `.next_u64() .below(n) .i64() .f64_bits() .f64_finite() .ascii_string(max) .utf8_string(max)`.\nFn aliases already defined: `FnStr FnStrN FnInt FnReal FnDelete FnDumps FnDumpb FnSize FnObjGet FnObjGetN FnObjSetNew FnObjSetNNew FnObjDel FnObjDelN FnArrGet FnArrSetNew FnArrAppendNew FnArrRemove FnIntVal FnRealVal FnStrVal FnTwoJson FnEqual FnCopy FnDeepCopy FnIter FnIterNext FnIterKey FnIterKeyLen FnIterValue`.\nDeclare any other signature you need locally in your own file.\n\n## FILE 1 — `tests/phase_b_value.rs`, covering CONFIGS.md rows 224-273\nRead `CONFIGS.md` rows 224-273 for the authoritative list. Label each `diff` with its row number.\n\nCover: fresh empty object; `json_object_set_new`/`setn_new` with keys of length 0,1,4,11,12,13,>24 (these hit distinct `hashlittle` alignment/tail paths — use `diff_n` with randomized keys of these lengths); `setn` with `key_len` shorter than `strlen`; keys containing an embedded NUL (ACCEPTED — NUL passes `utf8_check_string`); `*_nocheck` variants with invalid UTF-8 (accepted) vs `json_object_setn_new` with invalid UTF-8 (rejected -1); `value == object` self-insert (rejected -1); re-setting an existing key (value replaced, ordinal position preserved — verify by dumping); inserting the 8th then 9th distinct key (rehash boundary); `json_object_get`/`getn` hit/miss/`key_len` boundary/NUL key; `json_object_del`/`deln` hit(0)/miss(-1)/NUL key; `json_object_clear` then reuse; `json_object_update`, `_update_existing`, `_update_missing`, `_update_recursive` on `{\"a\":1,\"b\":2}` + `{\"b\":9,\"c\":3}` and on nested objects, plus object-vs-non-object mismatch, self-referential `other` (rejected), and non-object args (rejected); the whole iterator family (`json_object_iter`, `iter_next`, `iter_key`, `iter_key_len`, `iter_value`, `iter_at`, `key_to_iter`, `iter_set_new`) on empty/1-key/9+-key objects and with `iter == NULL`; arrays: fresh; append 1..8 then the 9th (grow to 16); `insert_new` at 0/middle/`index==entries`/`index>entries`(rejected); `set_new` valid and `index>=entries`(rejected); `remove` at 0/middle/last/out-of-range; `clear`; `extend` with `other` larger than 2x capacity, with an empty other, and with a non-array (rejected); `get` valid/`==entries`/huge; strings: `json_string`/`json_stringn` with \"\"/ASCII/2,3,4-byte UTF-8/embedded NUL, and invalid UTF-8 (rejected NULL); `*_nocheck` with invalid UTF-8 (accepted) — then `json_dumps` that invalid-UTF-8 string and compare (the C returns -1/NULL from `dump_string`); `json_string_set`/`setn`(+`_nocheck`) shorter/longer/NUL/invalid; `json_string_value`/`_length` on a string and a non-string; `json_integer`/`_set`/`_value` with 0,-1,LLONG_MIN,LLONG_MAX and wrong type; `json_real`/`_set`/`_value` with 0.0,-0.0,5e-324,DBL_MAX and NaN/±Inf (rejected); `json_number_value` on integer/real/non-number; the singletons `json_true`/`json_false`/`json_null` (assert `refcount == usize::MAX` and that decref is a no-op); `json_equal` on same pointer, type mismatch (integer 1 vs real 1.0 -> 0), NULL arg, and equal/unequal objects, arrays (order-sensitive), NUL-containing strings, integers, reals; `json_copy` on each type (shallow — assert children are the SAME pointer by comparing refcounts and dumps) and on a singleton (returns the same pointer); `json_deep_copy` on nested structures (children NOT shared) and on an indirect cycle (rejected NULL); `json_delete`/decref on each heap type and on singletons.\n\nInclude a `diff_n` with a fixed seed and >= 300 iterations that performs a randomized SEQUENCE of mutations (append/insert/remove/set/del/clear/update) on an object+array pair and after every step compares the full `json_dumps` (with and without SORT_KEYS), `json_object_size`/`json_array_size`, and the return codes. This finds ordering and grow/rehash bugs that single-call tests miss.\n\n## FILE 2 — `tests/phase_b_lowlevel.rs`, covering CONFIGS.md rows 274-313\nRead `CONFIGS.md` rows 274-313. These are the EXPORTED internal helpers; they are real exported symbols and must be called directly through the `.so`. Signatures (from `c_src/src/utf.h`, `strbuffer.h`, `hashtable.h`, `include/jansson_private.h` — read them to confirm):\n```c\nint   utf8_encode(int codepoint, char *buffer, size_t *size);\nsize_t utf8_check_first(char byte);\nsize_t utf8_check_full(const char *buffer, size_t size, int32_t *codepoint);\nconst char *utf8_iterate(const char *buffer, size_t bufsize, int32_t *codepoint);\nint   utf8_check_string(const char *string, size_t length);\n\nint   strbuffer_init(strbuffer_t *strbuff);      /* struct: { char *value; size_t length; size_t size; } */\nvoid  strbuffer_close(strbuffer_t *strbuff);\nvoid  strbuffer_clear(strbuffer_t *strbuff);\nconst char *strbuffer_value(const strbuffer_t *strbuff);\nchar *strbuffer_steal_value(strbuffer_t *strbuff);\nint   strbuffer_append_byte(strbuffer_t *strbuff, char byte);\nint   strbuffer_append_bytes(strbuffer_t *strbuff, const char *data, size_t size);\nchar  strbuffer_pop(strbuffer_t *strbuff);\n\nint   hashtable_init(hashtable_t *hashtable);\nvoid  hashtable_close(hashtable_t *hashtable);\nint   hashtable_set(hashtable_t *hashtable, const char *key, size_t key_len, json_t *value);\nvoid *hashtable_get(hashtable_t *hashtable, const char *key, size_t key_len);\nint   hashtable_del(hashtable_t *hashtable, const char *key, size_t key_len);\nvoid  hashtable_clear(hashtable_t *hashtable);\nvoid *hashtable_iter(hashtable_t *hashtable);\nvoid *hashtable_iter_at(hashtable_t *hashtable, const char *key, size_t key_len);\nvoid *hashtable_iter_next(hashtable_t *hashtable, void *iter);\nvoid *hashtable_iter_key(void *iter);\nsize_t hashtable_iter_key_len(void *iter);\nvoid *hashtable_iter_value(void *iter);\nvoid  hashtable_iter_set(void *iter, json_t *value);\n\nint   jsonp_strtod(strbuffer_t *strbuffer, double *out);\nint   jsonp_dtostr(char *buffer, size_t size, double value, int precision);\nchar *jsonp_strndup(const char *str, size_t len);\nvoid *jsonp_malloc(size_t size);\nvoid  jsonp_free(void *ptr);\nvoid *jsonp_realloc(void *ptr, size_t oldSize, size_t newSize);\nint   jsonp_loop_check(hashtable_t *parents, const json_t *json, char *key, size_t key_size, size_t *key_len_out);\nvoid  json_object_seed(size_t seed);\nconst char *jansson_version_str(void);\nint   jansson_version_cmp(int major, int minor, int micro);\nvoid  json_set_alloc_funcs(json_malloc_t, json_free_t);\nvoid  json_get_alloc_funcs(json_malloc_t*, json_free_t*);\nvoid  json_set_alloc_funcs2(json_malloc_t, json_realloc_t, json_free_t);\nvoid  json_get_alloc_funcs2(json_malloc_t*, json_realloc_t*, json_free_t*);\n```\n**You MUST read `c_src/src/strbuffer.h`, `c_src/src/hashtable.h`, `c_src/src/utf.h` and `c_src/include/jansson_private.h` and mirror the exact struct layouts with `#[repr(C)]`** (hashtable_t contains a `hashtable_list ordered_list`, `size_t size`, `bucket*`/`order` etc. — replicate it faithfully and assert your Rust struct's size matches by allocating generously). Also confirm the exact `jsonp_loop_check` and `jsonp_realloc` signatures in the header rather than trusting the sketch above.\n\nCover: `utf8_encode` at every length boundary (0, 0x7F/0x80, 0x7FF/0x800, 0xFFFF/0x10000, 0x10FFFF), on surrogates D800-DFFF (**ACCEPTED** — asymmetric with `utf8_check_full`), and `< 0` / `> 0x10FFFF` (rejected -1) — compare the emitted bytes AND `*size`; `utf8_check_first` for **all 256 byte values**; `utf8_check_full` on valid 2/3/4-byte sequences, size 1 and >4, overlong (E0 80 A9 / C0 80 / F0 80 80 80), surrogate (ED A0 80), value > 0x10FFFF (F4 BF BF BF), bad continuation bytes, and with `codepoint` NULL vs non-NULL; `utf8_iterate` with bufsize 0 (returns the buffer unchanged, does NOT write codepoint), 1-byte, 3-byte, truncated (NULL); `utf8_check_string` with length 0, ASCII, embedded NUL (valid), multi-byte, and truncated-by-length (invalid). Drive `utf8_check_full`/`utf8_iterate`/`utf8_check_string` with a `diff_n` over >= 2000 randomized byte strings (1-6 bytes, biased to include 0x80-0xFF lead bytes) — this is the highest-value test here.\nstrbuffer: init (assert size 16, length 0, `value[0]=='\\0'`); `append_byte` repeatedly crossing 16 -> 32 -> 64 (assert `size` and `length` after each); `append_bytes` with size 0 and sizes exactly filling the buffer; `pop` on non-empty and on EMPTY (returns `'\\0'`, length stays 0); `clear`; `value` fresh/populated/post-clear; `steal_value` then `close`.\nhashtable: init; `set` new/existing (value replaced, order kept)/8th/9th (rehash to order 4)/17th (order 5); `key_len` 0; keys with embedded NUL; `get` hit/miss/`key_len` mismatch on an equal prefix; `del` hit(0)/miss(-1)/sole entry/first/last; `clear` then reuse; the whole iterator family including `iter_at` and iterating to exhaustion. Add a `diff_n` (>= 300 iterations) doing randomized set/get/del/iterate sequences and comparing the full key order after each step.\n`jsonp_strtod` on integer/fraction/exponent text, `-0.0`, and a HUGE_VAL+ERANGE overflow (rejected -1); `jsonp_dtostr` with precision 0 and 1..17, buffers exactly large enough and too small (rejected -1), values straddling the `decpt <= -4` and `decpt > 16` exponent thresholds, and `-0.0` — plus a `diff_n` over >= 500 random doubles x precision 0..17 comparing the produced bytes and return code; `jsonp_strndup` with len 0 / < strlen / == strlen / data containing NULs; `jsonp_malloc(0)` (returns NULL by design) and non-zero; `jsonp_free(NULL)`; `jsonp_realloc` in both hook and emulation modes incl. newSize 0; `jsonp_loop_check` first visit (0) and repeat (-1); `json_object_seed` called twice (second ignored); the exported `hashtable_seed` variable read as non-zero after seeding; `json_set_alloc_funcs`/`json_set_alloc_funcs2` + the matching getters with all out-params non-NULL and each individually NULL; `jansson_version_str` == \"2.15.0\"; `jansson_version_cmp` for equal/lower/higher major, minor and micro.\n\n**CAUTION on allocator tests:** `json_set_alloc_funcs` changes GLOBAL state in the loaded library and will affect later tests in the same process. Put those in a test that RESTORES the original functions (fetch them with `json_get_alloc_funcs2` first, then restore) and keep the custom allocator a thin wrapper over `malloc`/`realloc`/`free` so nothing else breaks.\n\n## Verify before reporting\n```\ncd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-QfYM1w/translated_rust\ntimeout 600 cargo test --release --test phase_b_value 2>&1 | tail -40\ntimeout 600 cargo test --release --test phase_b_lowlevel 2>&1 | tail -40\n```\nBoth `.so` files alr