ER, _NULL_BYTE_IN_KEY, _DUPLICATE_KEY, _STACK_OVERFLOW, _END_OF_INPUT_EXPECTED`.\nAlso available: `dumps_to_string`, `decref`, `cs`, `cs_bytes`, `cstr_to_string`, `Rng::new(seed)`, `FnLoads`, `FnLoadb`.\n\n`load_then_dump` is the workhorse: it returns both the round-tripped value and the full error snapshot, so one call compares everything.\n\n## Scope: ERRORS.md rows 135-179 ONLY (lexer + parser; NOT the entry-point argument errors)\n**READ `ERRORS.md` rows 135-179 first** — it lists the exact trigger and exact expected C result (code + message) per row. Also read the \"Cross-cutting mechanics\" section at the top, which governs the messages:\n- **First error wins** (`jsonp_error_vset` returns early if `error->text[0] != 0`).\n- `error_set` appends `\" near '<saved_text>'\"` only when saved_text is non-empty AND length <= 20; longer tokens get the bare message.\n- With empty saved_text, `json_error_invalid_syntax` is PROMOTED to `json_error_premature_end_of_input` and `\" near end of file\"` is appended — unless the stream is in the UTF-8 error state (no suffix).\n\nWrite one `#[test]` fn per cluster; label every `diff` with its row number, e.g. `diff(\"row151/too big integer\", ...)`.\n\nCover:\n- rows 135-136 invalid UTF-8: lead bytes 0x80-0xBF, 0xC0, 0xC1, 0xF5-0xFF (loop over ALL of them), bad continuation bytes, overlong `\\xC0\\x80` / `\\xE0\\x80\\x80`, surrogate `\\xED\\xA0\\x80`, value > U+10FFFF `\\xF4\\xBF\\xBF\\xBF`, and a sequence truncated by end-of-input. Test these both inside a string and as a bare token. Note the message reports the LEAD byte and gets NO near-suffix.\n- rows 137-141: unterminated string `[\"abc`; raw newline inside a string; **every raw control byte 0x00..=0x1F individually inside a string** (0x0A is a different message from the rest); `\\u` with too few/non-hex digits; **every byte 0x20..=0x7E as the char after a backslash** (only `\"\\/bfnrtu` are valid).\n- rows 143-146 surrogates: high surrogate + a `\\u` that fails to decode; high + a non-low `\\uXXXX`; lone high (with and without a following char); lone low `\\uDC00`. Loop over several high/low values.\n- rows 148-154 numbers: leading zero `[01]`, `[-012]`; `-` with no digit `[-]`, `[-x]`, `[-.5]`; integer overflow at exactly `[9223372036854775808]` and `[-9223372036854775809]` (and confirm `[9223372036854775807]`/`[-9223372036854775808]` are ACCEPTED); `.` with no digit `[1.]`, `[1.e5]`; `e`/`E` with no digit `[1e]`, `[1e+]`, `[1E-x]`; real overflow `[1e999]`, `[-1e309]`. Also run the integer-overflow cases WITH `JSON_DECODE_INT_AS_REAL`, where they stop being errors.\n- rows 155-156: identifiers that are not exactly `true`/`false`/`null` (`nul`, `tru`, `TRUE`, `foo`, `nullx`, `truee`) and stray leading characters (single-quote, `#`, `@`, `+`, `.`, `*`, backtick, `;`).\n- rows 158, 160-163, 165-166 objects: non-string key `{1:2}`, `{,}`, `{\"a\":1,}`, `{:}`; **NUL byte in a key via a `\u0000` escape — must fail EVEN WITH `JSON_ALLOW_NUL`** (test with and without the flag); duplicate key with and without `JSON_REJECT_DUPLICATES`; missing `:` (`{\"a\" 1}`, `{\"a\",1}`); junk after a member (`{\"a\":1 \"b\":2}`); EOF before `}` (`{`, `{\"a\"`, `{\"a\":`, `{\"a\":1`, `{\"a\":1,`).\n- rows 168, 170-171 arrays: `[1,]`, `[,1]`, `[1 2]`, and EOF before `]` (`[`, `[1`, `[1,`).\n- **row 172 depth limit:** programmatically build N nested `[` (and separately N nested `{\"a\":`) and confirm the exact boundary — `JSON_PARSER_MAX_DEPTH` is 2048 and `lex->depth` increments per VALUE including scalars, so 2047 `[` plus an inner scalar is the maximum accepted and 2048 `[` plus a scalar fails with `JSON_ERROR_STACK_OVERFLOW` (\"maximum parsing depth reached\"). Determine the true boundary empirically from the C and assert BOTH the last accepted depth and the first rejected depth match between C and Rust.\n- row 173: `\u0000` in a string VALUE without `JSON_ALLOW_NUL` -> `json_error_null_character`; and WITH the flag -> accepted (assert the resulting string length is 3 for `\"a\u0000b\"`).\n- rows 175-176: `[}]`, `[,1]`, `[:]`, `[]]`, `{}}` and a bare `}`/`]`/`:`/`,`.\n- rows 177-179: root not a container without `JSON_DECODE_ANY` (`42`, `\"str\"`, `true`, `null`, `-1.5`); EMPTY input `\"\"`; trailing data without `JSON_DISABLE_EOF_CHECK` (`[1] [2]`, `{} x`, `[1]\"a\"`, `[1]]`).\n\nUse raw byte literals (`&b\"...\"[..]`) so you can embed arbitrary bytes; build control-byte cases programmatically with `Vec<u8>`.\n\nFinally add a `diff_n` with a fixed seed and **>= 2000 iterations** that generates random inputs — mutated valid JSON (flip/insert/delete a random byte), random ASCII, random bytes including 0x80-0xFF, and truncations of valid JSON — and feeds each to `json_loadb` under several flag combinations, comparing `(dump, ErrSnap)` each time. This fuzz sweep is the most valuable test in the file; make sure it runs and passes.\n\n## Verify before reporting\n```\ncd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-QfYM1w/translated_rust\ntimeout 600 cargo test --release --test phase_c_lex_errors 2>&1 | tail -40\n```\nBoth `.so` files already exist and are current; do NOT rebuild the C.\n\nReport: number of `#[test]` fns, the real `test result:` line, the depth boundary you measured, and for EVERY divergence the exact input bytes, flags, C result and Rust result."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":499,"cache_read_input_tokens":299167,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":499,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c1ef89ae-e696-4035-a7a9-923ffb551939","uuid":"d84a614e-9563-4069-97b2-230aefc672da","timestamp":"2026-08-14T15:22:28.013Z"}
