e`/`false`/`null` (`nul`, `tru`, `TRUE`, `foo`, `nullx`, `truee`) and stray leading characters (single-quote, `#`, `@`, `+`, `.`, `*`, backtick, `;`).\n- rows 158, 160-163, 165-166 objects: non-string key `{1:2}`, `{,}`, `{\"a\":1,}`, `{:}`; **NUL byte in a key via a `\u0000` escape — must fail EVEN WITH `JSON_ALLOW_NUL`** (test with and without the flag); duplicate key with and without `JSON_REJECT_DUPLICATES`; missing `:` (`{\"a\" 1}`, `{\"a\",1}`); junk after a member (`{\"a\":1 \"b\":2}`); EOF before `}` (`{`, `{\"a\"`, `{\"a\":`, `{\"a\":1`, `{\"a\":1,`).\n- rows 168, 170-171 arrays: `[1,]`, `[,1]`, `[1 2]`, and EOF before `]` (`[`, `[1`, `[1,`).\n- **row 172 depth limit:** programmatically build N nested `[` (and separately N nested `{\"a\":`) and confirm the exact boundary — `JSON_PARSER_MAX_DEPTH` is 2048 and `lex->depth` increments per VALUE including scalars, so 2047 `[` plus an inner scalar is the maximum accepted and 2048 `[` plus a scalar fails with `JSON_ERROR_STACK_OVERFLOW` (\"maximum parsing depth reached\"). Determine the true boundary empirically from the C and assert BOTH the last accepted depth and the first rejected depth match between C and Rust.\n- row 173: `\u0000` in a string VALUE without `JSON_ALLOW_NUL` -> `json_error_null_character`; and WITH the flag -> accepted (assert the resulting string length is 3 for `\"a\u0000b\"`).\n- rows 175-176: `[}]`, `[,1]`, `[:]`, `[]]`, `{}}` and a bare `}`/`]`/`:`/`,`.\n- rows 177-179: root not a container without `JSON_DECODE_ANY` (`42`, `\"str\"`, `true`, `null`, `-1.5`); EMPTY input `\"\"`; trailing data without `JSON_DISABLE_EOF_CHECK` (`[1] [2]`, `{} x`, `[1]\"a\"`, `[1]]`).\n\nUse raw byte literals (`&b\"...\"[..]`) so you can embed arbitrary bytes; build control-byte cases programmatically with `Vec<u8>`.\n\nFinally add a `diff_n` with a fixed seed and **>= 2000 iterations** that generates random inputs — mutated valid JSON (flip/insert/delete a random byte), random ASCII, random bytes including 0x80-0xFF, and truncations of valid JSON — and feeds each to `json_loadb` under several flag combinations, comparing `(dump, ErrSnap)` each time. This fuzz sweep is the most valuable test in the file; make sure it runs and passes.\n\n## Verify before reporting\n```\ncd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-QfYM1w/translated_rust\ntimeout 600 cargo test --release --test phase_c_lex_errors 2>&1 | tail -40\n```\nBoth `.so` files already exist and are current; do NOT rebuild the C.\n\nReport: number of `#[test]` fns, the real `test result:` line, the depth boundary you measured, and for EVERY divergence the exact input bytes, flags, C result and Rust result.","outputFile":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-QfYM1w/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-QfYM1w-translated-rust/c1ef89ae-e696-4035-a7a9-923ffb551939/tasks/a5315067f77468855.output","canReadOutputFile":true}}
