{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a351938deb540bcc4","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01JeVWYt1jVAYTTeEu5hYVcr","status":"completed","output_file":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-QfYM1w/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-QfYM1w-translated-rust/c1ef89ae-e696-4035-a7a9-923ffb551939/tasks/a351938deb540bcc4.output","summary":"All verification is green. Here is the report.\n\n## Sites changed (9 fixes across 6 files)\n\n**Found by an empirical differential sweep, not by reading alone.** I built three hostile-input drivers in `/tmp` that `dlopen` both `cbuild/libjansson.so` and a `-C overflow-checks=on` Rust build, call the exported symbols (including the private `jsonp_*`/`dtoa_*`/`utf8_*`/`strbuffer_*`/`hashtable_*` ones) with extreme arguments, and run each case in a forked child so an abort doesn't stop the sweep. Two of the three fixes below were **genuine panics the existing 164-test suite does not reach**.\n\n### Real panics reproduced against the C\n\n1. **`src/dtoa.rs:2006`** — `*decpt = k + 1` → `k.wrapping_add(1)`\n   C (`dtoa.c:6232`): `*decpt = k + 1;`. The `no_digits` state sets `k = -1 - ndigits`, so `dtoa_r(1.0, 3, INT_MIN, ...)` arrives with `k == INT_MAX`. C wraps and reports `*decpt == -2147483648`; Rust panicked with *\"attempt to add with overflow\"*. Verified: C prints `dp=-2147483648`, Rust now matches.\n\n2. **`src/load.rs:1365`** — `(*stream).data[(*stream).pos]` → `*(*stream).data.as_ptr().add((*stream).pos)`\n   C (`load.c`, `callback_get`): `c = stream->data[stream->pos];`, an unchecked read. `stream->len` is whatever the caller's `json_load_callback_t` returned and only `0`/`(size_t)-1` are rejected, so a callback returning more than `MAX_BUF_LEN` (e.g. `buflen+1`) leaves `pos >= 1024`. C reads into the adjacent struct fields and continues; Rust panicked *\"index out of bounds\"*. **Note: slice bounds checks are on in every profile, so this aborted the process even in the shipped release build** — it was not gated behind overflow-checks.\n\n3. **`src/strbuffer.rs:92`** — `(*strbuff).size - (*strbuff).length` → `.wrapping_sub(...)`\n   C (`strbuffer.c:61`): unguarded `size_t` subtraction. `strbuffer_append_bytes` is exported and takes a caller-owned `strbuffer_t`; with `length > size` the C wraps to a huge value and simply skips the grow branch. Rust panicked *\"attempt to subtract with overflow\"*.\n\n### Same class, C is unguarded, reachable in principle\n\n4. **`src/dtoa.rs:1158,1160`** — `i = ndigits + k + 1` / `ilim1 = i - 1` → wrapping (C `dtoa.c:5330-5333`). With `ndigits` near `INT_MAX` the C wraps negative, which then trips its own `if (i <= 0) i = 1;` clamp.\n5. **`src/dump.rs:59,67`** — `buf->used + size` / `used += size` → wrapping (C `dump.c:45,48`).\n6. **`src/dump.rs:258`** — `k1->len - k2->len` → `wrapping_sub` (C `dump.c:212`); `len` is a caller key length truncated `size_t`→`int`.\n7. **`src/value.rs:642,648-651`** — `entries + amount`, `max(size + amount, size * 2)` → wrapping (C `value.c:514,519`).\n8. **`src/load.rs:272,277,293,296`** — `stream->line/column` `++`/`--` → wrapping (C `load.c`). Plain `int`s bumped once per input newline/char; a >2 GB input wraps them in C.\n9. **`src/pack_unpack.rs:116,127,130`** — `s->line/column++` → wrapping (C `pack_unpack.c:68,75,79`). Same, driven by a caller-supplied format string.\n\n## Deliberately left alone\n\n- **`strbuffer.rs` `SIZE_MAX` guards and `size*2` / `length+size+1`** — these *are* the C's own guards (`strbuffer.c:66-69`). C's left-to-right short-circuit means each is evaluated only after the previous ruled out the wrap: `size > SIZE_MAX-1` rejects `SIZE_MAX` before `SIZE_MAX-1-size` runs, and `length > SIZE_MAX-1-size` bounds `length+size+1` by exactly `SIZE_MAX`. Verified tight by driving `append_bytes` with `SIZE_MAX`, `-2`, `-3`, `SIZE_MAX/2`, `SIZE_MAX/2+1`. Plain operators, per instructions.\n- **`hashtable.rs:255-260` `init_pair`** — the C's `key_len >= (size_t)-1 - offsetof(pair_t,key)` guard makes `offsetof + key_len + 1 <= SIZE_MAX` exactly. Swept `key_len` at `-1,-2,-40,-47,-48,-49` via both `hashtable_set` and `json_object_setn_new_nocheck`.\n- **`strconv.rs` `exp = decpt - 1`, `vdigits_start`, `3 + (vdigits_end - vdigits_start) + …`** — `jsonp_dtostr` only ever calls `dtoa_r` with mode 0 or 2, for which the `no_digits` path (the only source of extreme `decpt`) is unreachable, so `decpt ∈ {1, 9999} ∪ [-323,310]`. Confirmed by ~96 000 `jsonp_dtostr` calls spanning `precision` from `INT_MIN` to `INT_MAX` × 10 buffer sizes × random doubles — byte-identical, no panic.\n- **`dump.rs` `dump_indent` `depth as usize * ws_count`** — C computes this in 32-bit `unsigned int`; Rust in `usize`. Divergence needs `depth ≥ 1.4e8`, and `do_dump` is recursive so the stack dies first. Cannot panic (product < 2^36).\n- **`dump.rs:144` `lim as usize - pos as usize`** — `pos <= lim` holds structurally: `utf8_iterate` returns `pos + count` only after rejecting `count > bufsize`.\n- **`utf.rs`** — `utf8_encode` guards `< 0` and `> 0x10FFFF`; `utf8_check_full` returns 0 unless `size ∈ {2,3,4}` so `(value << 6) + …` stays under 2^23; `utf8_check_string`'s `length - i` has `i < length`. Swept all 256 first bytes and `size`/`bufsize` at 0..6, 100, `SIZE_MAX`, `SIZE_MAX-1`.\n- **`lookup3.rs:6` `rot`: `x >> (32 - k)`** — would panic at `k == 0`, but every `mix`/`final` call site uses a nonzero literal.\n- **`dtoa.rs` variable shifts** — audited each: `lshift` guards `kk != 0` so `32 - kk ∈ 1..31`; `b2d`/`d2b` shift counts are bounded by `hi0bits`/`lo0bits` ∈ 0..31 with the `k < EBITS` / `kk != 0` splits; the file's `sl`/`sr` helpers already use `wrapping_shl`/`wrapping_shr`. `mult`'s `(*x as u64)*y + *xc + carry` hits exactly `u64::MAX` at worst — no overflow.\n- **`dtoa.rs:807,839` `*bxe / (*sxe + 1)`, `q += 1`** — C would divide by zero here, i.e. SIGFPE, not a wrapped result, so there is no C behaviour to reproduce. Never hit in any sweep.\n- **`load.rs`/`pack_unpack.rs` `size_t` counters** (`stream->position`, `s->pos`) — would need 2^64 increments.\n- **`pack_unpack.rs:72` `type_names[json_typeof(x) as usize]`** — an unchecked C read that Rust bounds-checks, but reaching it requires a fabricated `json_t` with an out-of-range type tag, which is already UB in C (it indexes past `const char *const[8]` and passes the garbage to `%s`). C has no defined result to match, so I left the checked index rather than trade a deterministic panic for UB. Documented rather than changed.\n\n## Verification results (actual output)\n\n```\n$ timeout 600 cargo build 2>&1 | tail -5\n    Finished `dev` profile [unoptimized + debuginfo] target(s) in 0.01s\n\n$ timeout 600 cargo build --release 2>&1 | tail -5\n    Finished `release` profile [optimized] target(s) in 0.01s\n```\n\n`cargo test` (debug, overflow-checks ON) — 12/12 binaries, 164 tests:\n```\ntest result: ok. 0 passed; ...   test result: ok. 5 passed; ...   test result: ok. 22 passed; ...\ntest result: ok. 20 passed; ...  test result: ok. 18 passed; ...  test result: ok. 20 passed; ...\ntest result: ok. 3 passed; ...   test result: ok. 15 passed; ...  test result: ok. 15 passed; ...\ntest result: ok. 21 passed; ...  test result: ok. 14 passed; ...  test result: ok. 11 passed; ...\n```\n`cargo test --release` — identical: 12/12 `ok`, 164 tests. No `panicked at src/`, no `attempt to` in either.\n\n**The headline sweep — `RUSTFLAGS=\"-C overflow-checks=on\" cargo test --release`:**\n```\ntest result: ok. 0 passed; 0 failed; ...      test result: ok. 5 passed; 0 failed; ...\ntest result: ok. 22 passed; 0 failed; ...     test result: ok. 20 passed; 0 failed; ...\ntest result: ok. 18 passed; 0 failed; ...     test result: ok. 20 passed; 0 failed; ...\ntest result: ok. 3 passed; 0 failed; ...      test result: ok. 15 passed; 0 failed; ...\ntest result: ok. 15 passed; 0 failed; ...     test result: ok. 21 passed; 0 failed; ...\ntest result: ok. 14 passed; 0 failed; ...   