{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_ytufjw4bjnhawqxgsre45fml76inj32skohfiz52q7dp7ub2i46a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. Final state:\n\n## Completion gate\n\n| Gate | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md`: 0 missing/undefined non-libc symbols | ✅ C=130, Rust=130, 0 missing; undefined set is libc/unwind only |\n| Phase B: every `CONFIGS.md` row passes across randomized inputs | ✅ 313/313 rows checked off |\n| Binary stdout matches byte-for-byte | ✅ `diff_driver` — 75,420 lines identical |\n| Phase C: every `ERRORS.md` row has a passing test | ✅ 373 rows (296 TEST + 58 OOM tested; 19 UB/INT/DEAD documented as unreachable with reasons) |\n| Holds under every feature combination | ✅ `Cargo.toml` has **no** `[features]`, so exactly one configuration exists — plus verified under debug and `-C overflow-checks=on` |\n\n**164 tests across 12 binaries**, green in release, debug, and overflow-checks-on release.\n\n## Real bugs found and fixed\n\n**1. `dtoa_r` translated the wrong `#ifdef` branch.** Symbol parity was *already* satisfied — this is exactly why it's necessary but not sufficient. `src/dtoa.rs` implemented the classic Bigint path and its header comment asserted that was byte-identical to the real one. But `dtoa.c:385` defines `USE_BF96`, so C compiles a completely different exact-integer algorithm. The tell: `cargo build` reported `PTEN`, `LHINT`, `PFIVEBITS` as *never used* — precisely the tables the real path needs. Invisible via `json_dumps` (which only uses modes 0/2); appeared only when the exported `dtoa_r` was called in modes 1/4/5. Retranslated from the preprocessed active C: **232 → 0** differing driver lines.\n\n**2. Five confirmed \"Rust panics where C wraps\" divergences** — a panic across FFI aborts, which C never does. Most notably `load.rs`'s `stream->data[stream->pos]`: a `json_load_callback_t` returning more than `MAX_BUF_LEN` makes C do an unchecked read, while Rust's bounds check **aborted even in the shipped release build** (not gated behind overflow-checks). Four more sites hardened where C is likewise unguarded; sites where C's own guards make overflow impossible were deliberately left alone.\n\n## Things I corrected rather than accepted\n\nSeveral apparent \"divergences\" were test defects, and I verified each against C before believing it:\n- A `{s:s}` unpack read a **borrowed** pointer after `decref` — use-after-free producing per-heap garbage.\n- A vararg misalignment passed a key to `\"O\"`/`\"o\"`, so the library reinterpreted a `char*` as a `json_t*` — the test wasn't exercising NULL rejection at all.\n- My own boundary test self-inserted into a refcount-1 container; C's `json == value` path calls `json_decref(value)`, **consuming the reference and freeing the container**, so everything after was UB. Now increfs first and pins that behavior.\n- My OOM test passed a non-NUL-terminated Rust `String` to the `strlen`-based `json_object_set_new`, causing a flake (**2–4/25 → 0/25** after fixing).\n\nTwo things worth flagging: I hardened the harness `decref` to *assert* on over-decref rather than wrap silently — that's what surfaced two of the above. And the harness now picks the `.so` matching the test profile deterministically instead of \"newest file\", which was silently swapping which library was under test during concurrent builds.\n\nThree `CONFIGS.md`/`ERRORS.md` items are documented as genuinely unreachable rather than quietly checked off — e.g. `init_pair`'s `key_len` overflow guard sits *behind* `hash_str`, so any value large enough to trip it segfaults both libraries first."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":704,"cache_read_input_tokens":426513,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":704,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c1ef89ae-e696-4035-a7a9-923ffb551939","uuid":"d265de6e-69c0-4719-b136-13ba87a595f3","timestamp":"2026-08-14T16:20:57.913Z"}
